 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "We can plot the fraction molecules in each vibrational state",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "vibrational-populations",
   "prompt": "<p>Use $\\kThetavib = 805$ K for Cl₂ from Table 18.4.1. At 300 K, with the zero of energy at $v = 0$, find (a) $\\kqvib$, (b) the fraction $f_0$ of molecules in $v = 0$, (c) the fraction $f_1$ in $v = 1$, and (d) vibration's contribution to the molar heat capacity $\\kCVbar$, in J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 1.073,
      "hint": "Every expression needs only $e^{-\\Theta_\\text{vib}/T} = e^{-805/300}$."
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 0.932
     },
     {
      "part": "c",
      "value": 0.0637
     },
     {
      "part": "d",
      "value": 4.71,
      "unit": "J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹"
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>$\\kThetavib/\\kT = 805/300 = 2.683$ and $e^{-2.683} = 0.0683$. (a) $\\kqvib = 1/(1 - 0.0683) = 1.073$. (b) By Equation 18.4.6, $f_0 = 1 - 0.0683 = 0.932$. (c) $f_1 = (0.932)(0.0683) = 0.0637$. (d) Per mole, Equation 18.4.5 times $N_A$ gives $\\kCVbar = R(2.683)^2(0.0683)/(0.932)^2 = 0.567R = 4.71$ J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Cl₂'s vibration is partly awake at room temperature, though 93% of the molecules are still in $v = 0$.</p>"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "source_id": "Compare the parameters on Table 18.4.1",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "vibrational-ladder",
   "prompt": "<p>N₂ has $\\kThetavib = 3393$ K (Table 18.4.1). At 1000 K, with the zero of energy at $v = 0$, find (a) $\\kqvib$ and (b) the fraction of molecules in $v = 0$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 1.035,
      "hint": "Use Equation 18.4.3 with $h\\nu/kT = \\Theta_\\text{vib}/T$."
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 0.966
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>$\\kThetavib/\\kT = 3393/1000 = 3.393$ and $e^{-3.393} = 0.0336$. (a) $\\kqvib = 1/(1 - 0.0336) = 1.035$. (b) $f_0 = 1 - 0.0336 = 0.966$. Even at 1000 K, 97% of N₂ molecules are in the ground vibrational state.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
